1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:07,425
A household in Ireland pays 39 cents for every kilowatt-hour of electricity.

2
00:00:07,425 --> 00:00:09,200
In Hungary, 11 cents.

3
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:13,750
Same electricity, more than three times the price.

4
00:00:14,950 --> 00:00:20,013
So how did Europe's power bills get here? Here's every price since 2008,

5
00:00:20,013 --> 00:00:23,600
the year they exploded, and why the gap is so wide.

6
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:27,419
First, what we're measuring.

7
00:00:27,419 --> 00:00:33,045
These are Eurostat's prices for what it calls a medium-sized household:

8
00:00:33,045 --> 00:00:37,800
one that uses between 2,500 and 5,000 kilowatt-hours a year.

9
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:43,825
A kilowatt-hour is what a 1,000-watt heater uses in an hour.

10
00:00:44,825 --> 00:00:48,273
Every tax and charge is included, and since 2021,

11
00:00:48,273 --> 00:00:52,917
so are the discounts and credits governments gave to soften bills.

12
00:00:52,917 --> 00:00:58,686
The prices are in euros, every six months, and they're not adjusted for inflation.

13
00:00:58,686 --> 00:01:00,375
We'll come back to that.

14
00:01:01,975 --> 00:01:06,825
The first half of 2008. The EU average is 16 cents.

15
00:01:07,825 --> 00:01:15,125
Denmark is the most expensive, at 26 cents. Then Germany, at 21, and Italy, at 20.

16
00:01:16,125 --> 00:01:22,048
The cheapest is Bulgaria, at 7 cents, then Estonia and Latvia, at 8. And Hungary,

17
00:01:22,048 --> 00:01:27,825
today the cheapest in the EU? Back then it paid 15 cents, close to the average.

18
00:01:29,025 --> 00:01:32,377
For the next thirteen years, prices creep up slowly,

19
00:01:32,377 --> 00:01:35,600
by about two and a half percent a year on average.

20
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:44,578
By the first half of 2021, the EU average is 22 cents.

21
00:01:44,578 --> 00:01:51,400
Germany is now the most expensive, at 32, with Denmark close behind at 29.

22
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:53,191
In Germany,

23
00:01:53,191 --> 00:01:58,944
a big part of that price was a surcharge that paid for new wind and solar power.

24
00:01:58,944 --> 00:02:04,625
It was scrapped in July 2022, but by then, something much bigger was happening.

25
00:02:06,225 --> 00:02:09,669
Gas prices had been climbing through 2021.

26
00:02:09,669 --> 00:02:15,900
After Russia's invasion of Ukraine in February 2022, they rose much further.

27
00:02:18,900 --> 00:02:24,339
In much of Europe, gas power stations set the wholesale price of electricity,

28
00:02:24,339 --> 00:02:26,600
so power prices followed gas up.

29
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:33,420
In the second half of 2022, a Danish household pays 59 cents a kilowatt-hour.

30
00:02:33,420 --> 00:02:37,350
That's twice what it paid a year and a half earlier.

31
00:02:38,350 --> 00:02:42,350
Belgium pays 45 cents. Italy, 36.

32
00:02:43,750 --> 00:02:46,731
But not everyone felt it the same way.

33
00:02:46,731 --> 00:02:52,064
France capped the rise in its regulated prices at 4 percent in 2022,

34
00:02:52,064 --> 00:02:54,025
so its line barely moves.

35
00:02:55,025 --> 00:03:00,393
Spain and Portugal capped the price of the gas used to make electricity.

36
00:03:00,393 --> 00:03:05,313
Spanish prices still jumped, to 34 cents, but they fell back fast,

37
00:03:05,313 --> 00:03:07,550
to 25 cents within six months.

38
00:03:08,550 --> 00:03:13,687
And in Hungary, household prices have been set by the state since 2013.

39
00:03:13,687 --> 00:03:17,450
Its line stays almost flat through the whole crisis.

40
00:03:18,850 --> 00:03:24,350
Gas prices fell back during 2023. But the EU average didn't.

41
00:03:27,350 --> 00:03:32,803
It stayed high, and reached its highest point in the first half of 2024,

42
00:03:32,803 --> 00:03:33,712
at 29 cents.

43
00:03:33,712 --> 00:03:40,150
Many contracts and regulated prices take months or years to catch up with the market.

44
00:03:41,150 --> 00:03:43,950
Since then, it has hardly come down.

45
00:03:45,150 --> 00:03:49,752
In the first half of 2026, the EU average is 28 cents,

46
00:03:49,752 --> 00:03:52,650
less than one cent below the peak.

47
00:03:53,650 --> 00:03:56,635
Denmark is the exception. In January,

48
00:03:56,635 --> 00:04:01,152
it cut its electricity tax almost to zero for two years.

49
00:04:01,152 --> 00:04:05,750
Its price fell from 33 cents to 24 in a single half-year.

50
00:04:07,350 --> 00:04:14,712
Here's the full ranking for early 2026. The most expensive is Ireland, at 39 cents.

51
00:04:14,712 --> 00:04:19,325
Then Germany and Belgium, at 36, and Austria, at 33.

52
00:04:20,325 --> 00:04:24,600
Then Italy and Sweden, at 31, and Poland, at 30.

53
00:04:25,600 --> 00:04:30,847
In the middle are Spain and France, at 25 cents, and the Netherlands,

54
00:04:30,847 --> 00:04:32,900
Greece and Portugal, at 24.

55
00:04:33,900 --> 00:04:39,697
The cheapest are Bulgaria and Malta, at 13 cents, and Hungary, at 11.

56
00:04:39,697 --> 00:04:44,150
Outside the EU, Norway pays 20 cents, and Iceland 21.

57
00:04:45,550 --> 00:04:51,478
Poland is the one to watch. In late 2022 it paid 16 cents. Now it pays 30,

58
00:04:51,478 --> 00:04:55,885
almost twice as much. Part of that is a stronger zloty,

59
00:04:55,885 --> 00:04:59,650
which makes Polish prices look higher in euros.

60
00:05:00,650 --> 00:05:05,576
Romania jumped too. Its price cap ended in July 2025,

61
00:05:05,576 --> 00:05:09,200
and its price went from 19 cents to 29.

62
00:05:10,800 --> 00:05:17,403
So how much dearer is electricity than in 2008? Comparing whole years,

63
00:05:17,403 --> 00:05:24,950
the EU average rose from 16 cents in 2008 to 29 in 2025. That's 76 percent more.

64
00:05:25,950 --> 00:05:31,036
But prices in general rose too, by 47 percent over the same years.

65
00:05:31,036 --> 00:05:36,970
So after inflation, electricity costs about a fifth more than it did in 2008.

66
00:05:36,970 --> 00:05:41,825
A real rise, but not the near-doubling the raw numbers suggest.

67
00:05:43,425 --> 00:05:49,002
Why does one country pay three times as much as another? Every bill has three parts:

68
00:05:49,002 --> 00:05:53,650
the energy itself, the network that brings it to your home, and taxes.

69
00:05:54,650 --> 00:06:02,109
Across the EU in 2025, the energy was about 12 cents a kilowatt-hour, the network 8,

70
00:06:02,109 --> 00:06:03,175
and taxes 8.

71
00:06:04,175 --> 00:06:07,145
In Denmark, taxes were almost half the bill.

72
00:06:07,145 --> 00:06:11,128
That's why cutting them made such a difference. In Germany,

73
00:06:11,128 --> 00:06:14,975
the network and taxes together cost more than the energy.

74
00:06:15,975 --> 00:06:19,742
In Hungary, the energy part was just two and a half cents,

75
00:06:19,742 --> 00:06:22,925
because the state sets the price, not the market.

76
00:06:23,925 --> 00:06:25,436
Ireland is different.

77
00:06:25,436 --> 00:06:31,048
Its government gave households credits that more than cancelled out the taxes.

78
00:06:31,048 --> 00:06:35,509
But the energy part alone was 23 cents, the highest in the EU.

79
00:06:35,509 --> 00:06:40,762
Ireland makes much of its electricity from gas, so when gas is expensive,

80
00:06:40,762 --> 00:06:41,625
so is power.

81
00:06:43,225 --> 00:06:46,766
What does that mean for a year's electricity?

82
00:06:46,766 --> 00:06:50,700
Take a home that uses 3,500 kilowatt-hours a year.

83
00:06:51,700 --> 00:07:00,697
At the EU average price, that's about 990 euros. In Ireland, about 1,355.

84
00:07:00,697 --> 00:07:05,750
In Germany, 1,266. In Hungary, about 400.

85
00:07:06,750 --> 00:07:12,570
The thin bars show the same home in 2008. The Irish bill has more than doubled.

86
00:07:12,570 --> 00:07:17,137
The Hungarian one is lower than it was, even before inflation.

87
00:07:17,137 --> 00:07:18,905
These are illustrations:

88
00:07:18,905 --> 00:07:23,325
real bills add standing charges and depend on your contract.

89
00:07:24,725 --> 00:07:28,923
And cheap in euros isn't always cheap for the people paying.

90
00:07:28,923 --> 00:07:33,191
Bulgaria has some of the lowest electricity prices in the EU,

91
00:07:33,191 --> 00:07:35,500
and also the lowest minimum wage.

92
00:07:36,500 --> 00:07:42,475
We looked at what wages really buy in our video on minimum wages across the EU.

93
00:07:44,075 --> 00:07:50,446
Three things to take away. One: Ireland now pays the most for electricity in the EU,

94
00:07:50,446 --> 00:07:55,300
39 cents a kilowatt-hour, more than three times Hungary's price.

95
00:07:56,300 --> 00:08:02,970
Two: the 2022 shock never fully went away. The EU average is still 28 cents,

96
00:08:02,970 --> 00:08:04,550
close to its peak.

97
00:08:05,550 --> 00:08:05,955
Three:

98
00:08:05,955 --> 00:08:12,230
where your country sits on this chart depends less on the weather than on politics and pipes.

99
00:08:12,230 --> 00:08:16,750
Taxes, price caps and how much gas you burn decide most of the gap.

100
00:08:17,950 --> 00:08:24,600
Next on Terrachart: Europe's biggest populations, from 1950 to 2100.

101
00:08:25,800 --> 00:08:30,707
Sources are in the description. What do you pay per kilowatt-hour where you live?

102
00:08:30,707 --> 00:08:33,675
Check your last bill and tell us in the comments.
